"Introducing Claude Opus 5.5 \\ Anthropic (https://www.anthropic.com/claude-opus-5-5)\nciteturn1132view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1131view0\",\"pattern\":\"40% less\"}); Total lines: 440\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content cite1†Skip to footer L1: \nL2:   * Research\nL3:   * cite2†Policy L4:   * Commitments\nL5:   * Learn\nL6:   * cite3†News L7: \nL8: cite4†Try Claude†claude.ai L9: \nL10:   1. cite5†Introduction L11:   2. cite6†Performance and cost-effectiveness L12:   3. cite7†Safety L13:   4. cite8†Availability L14: # Claude Opus 5.5\nL15: \nL16: September 22, 2026\nL17: \nL18:   1. cite5†(1)Introduction L19:   2. cite6†(2)Performance and cost L20:   3. cite7†(3)Safety L21:   4. cite8†(4)Availability L22: \nL23: [Button: Scroll down]\nL24: \nL25: We’re introducing Claude Opus 5.5, the first model in our new Claude 5.5 family. It performs at the level of Claude Fable 5.1 on most work and costs 40% less to run than Opus 5.\nL26: Claude Opus 5.5 is our first release since we called for cite9†pacing the frontier†darioamodei.com . It was tested before release by external evaluators, including cite10†Frontier Design†www.imaginefrontier.com and cite11†METR†metr.org . On our automated behavioral audit, the most comprehensive alignment test we run, Opus 5.5 is the strongest-performing model we’ve tested to date. It also comes with the safeguards we’ve developed for our most capable models.\nL27: Here are some of the improvements you can expect from Opus 5.5:\nL28: Performance. Opus 5.5 is a major step up from Opus 5. It’s the new leading model, and early testers saw large jumps in performance on their most complex work. One tester completed a 680,000-line code migration in less than a day—work that would have taken an engineering team weeks.\nL29: It’s good at finding and fixing inefficiencies in software: when we asked it to cut load times across every page of a web app, Opus 5.5 succeeded 39 of 40 times, while Opus 5 made smaller improvements that also altered the app’s behavior. A different tester had several Claude models build a game from a single prompt; Opus 5.5 scored higher than any other model on the strength of its graphics and polish.\nL30: Safety. Opus 5.5 achieves the best scores of any model to date on our automated behavioral audit, our alignment suite that tests Claude across thousands of simulated scenarios. It is much less likely than recent models to take hard-to-reverse actions or act outside the boundaries it’s been given, and it’s more resistant than Opus 5 to prompt injection. We’ve also broadened our alignment testing to cover longer tasks, impossible tasks, and scenarios modeled on real incidents, though it still has limits.\nL31: Full details of our evaluation are available in the cite12†Opus 5.5 System Card†anthropic.com .\nL32: Because Opus 5.5 is comparable to Claude Mythos 5.1 in biology and cybersecurity, we’re deploying it with safeguards similar to those on Claude Fable 5.1. Vetted organizations can apply today to our cite13†Life Sciences Verification Program to use Opus 5.5 for biology research. In the coming weeks we will also be expanding access to our cite14†Cyber Verification Program†support.claude.com , and verified cybersecurity practitioners will be able to use Opus 5.5 for their work.\nL33: Cost and speed. Opus 5.5 requires less compute to serve than Opus 5, and its pricing reflects that. Our tests show that at default settings it will cost 40% less than Opus 5 on typical workloads. Input and output tokens are $4 and $20 per million, 20% less than Opus 5. Cache reads (which make up the majority of agentic and coding work costs) are $0.20 per million tokens, 60% less than Opus 5. Opus 5.5 also generates output more than 30% faster than Opus 5.\nL34: In addition to the price drop, we’re increasing five-hour usage limits on Pro, Max, Team, and seat-based Enterprise plans. We’re also providing subscription users a rate limit reset, which you can now save and use whenever you choose.\nL35: Communication. Opus 5.5 communicates more naturally than prior models. Early testers found its writing clearer and easier to follow, which addresses some of the common feedback we heard about Opus 5. It puts the most important information up front, and its style makes it a better work partner over long sessions. As one early tester put it, “it writes the way I do.” In our own use, this has made Opus 5.5’s work easier to follow and check—which is a safety benefit as well as a practical one.\nL36: Claude Sonnet 5.5 and Claude Haiku 5.5 will follow in the coming weeks, with many of the same improvements to performance, efficiency, and safety.\nL37: ## Performance and cost-effectiveness\nL38: \nL39: On our benchmarks, Claude Opus 5.5 leads in agentic coding, computer use, and knowledge work. That said, at these levels of capability we’ve found that benchmark margins have become a less reliable guide to real-world differences. In our own use, the gap between Opus 5.5 and Claude Fable 5.1 is narrower than these scores suggest.\nL40:  | Opus 5.5  | Fable 5.1  | Opus 5  | GPT-6 Astra  | GPT-5.6 Sol\nL41: Agentic coding Terminal-Bench 4.0¹  |\nL42: --- | ---\nL43: Agentic coding Terminal-Bench 4.0¹  | 66.4%  | 55.8%  | 52.3%  | 57.9%  | 37.3%\nL44: Agentic coding FrontierCode v1.1 (Main)  |\nL45: --- | ---\nL46: Agentic coding FrontierCode v1.1 (Main)  | 54.4%  | 50.3%  | 48.0%  | 53.3%  | 47.5%\nL47: Agentic coding CursorBench 4.0  |\nL48: --- | ---\nL49: Agentic coding CursorBench 4.0  | 57.8%  | 51.8%  | 46.6%  | —  | 41.7%\nL50: Knowledge work GDPval-AA v2.1  |\nL51: --- | ---\nL52: Knowledge work GDPval-AA v2.1  | 1846  | 1735  | 1708  | 1542  | 1588\nL53: Business workflows AutomationBench²  |\nL54: --- | ---\nL55: Business workflows AutomationBench²  | 40.0%  | 31.4%  | 26.9%  | 41.4%  | 28.8%\nL56: Multidisciplinary reasoning Humanity's Last Exam  |\nL57: --- | ---\nL58: Multidisciplinary reasoning Humanity's Last Exam  | 67.7%with tools  | 65.6%with tools  | 63.6%with tools  | 57.2%with tools  | —\nL59: Agentic scientific research Terminal-Bench-Science 0.1³  |\nL60: --- | ---\nL61: Agentic scientific research Terminal-Bench-Science 0.1³  | 58.7%  | 52.6%  | 29.0%  | 64.6%  | 22.4%\nL62: Computer use OSWorld 2.0  |\nL63: --- | ---\nL64: Computer use OSWorld 2.0  | 81.8%partial  | 80.7%partial  | 74.0%partial  | —  | —\nL65: Visual chart recognition Chartography  |\nL66: --- | ---\nL67: Visual chart recognition Chartography  | 89.0%with tools  | 88.4%with tools  | 83.4%with tools  | —  | —\nL369: > “Verbose, hard-to-follow output has been my biggest frustration with frontier models, and Claude Opus 5.5 fixes it. It writes like a good colleague, and follows our writing rules. A design spec came out usable with very minimal edits, and when it rewrote one of our prompts I preferred its version to my own. When it optimized our test suite, I could follow its reasoning easily and shipped the change with confidence.”\nL370: \nL371: Company Ramp\nL372: \nL373: Author John Ruelas, Staff Software Engineer\nL374: \nL375: Quote\nL376: > “I run long Claude Code sessions every day. On a multi-day rebase of 40 stacked pull requests, one Claude Opus 5.5 session directed a dozen more sessions and laid out every conflict plainly. On the calls that it held, it framed them clearly that after hours away I could answer in minutes. All 40 passed CI the next afternoon. It’s a substantial upgrade over Opus 5.”\nL377: \nL378: Company Stripe\nL379: \nL380: Author Cristian Rivera, Staff Software Engineer\nL381: \nL382: Quote\nL383: > “Our customers use Box AI on enormous amounts of content, so speed and cost are a top priority. In our evaluations, Claude Opus 5.5 used a third of the tokens Opus 5 did, and its answers were 40% less verbose without losing accuracy. We expect that to matter a lot for teams running agents across their content in areas like financial services and the public sector.”\nL384: \nL385: Company Box\nL386: \nL387: Author Yashodha Bhavnani, VP of AI Products\nL388: \nL389: Quote\nL390: > “Overnight, Claude Opus 5.5 autonomously handled a bug in our Lakehouse services layer that I hadn’t had time to diagnose. It investigated, designed the fix, and implemented it on its own. By morning the change was done and passed our test suite. Its writing is easy to follow and more coherent than Opus 5’s. Our pull requests and user-facing docs have needed almost no editing.”\nL391: \nL392: Company Chicago Trading Company\nL393: \nL394: Author Austen Tomek, Principal Engineer\nL395: \nL396: Quote\nL397: > “Claude Opus 5.5 is the first model we’d default to at medium effort. In our testing it matched Opus 5 on high effort, while using 20 to 25% fewer output tokens. On long, messy investigations it always came back with a clear, actionable answer. This means our customers get more done for less.”\nL398: \nL399: Company Factory\nL400: \nL401: Author Zimu Li, Member of Technical Staff\nL402: ## Safety\nL403: ### Pacing the frontier\nL404: \nL405: Last week, our CEO, Dario Amodei, argued that cite9†AI progress should be paced†darioamodei.com so that safety practices stay ahead of model capabilities. Pacing is an approach to keeping AI safe, remaining competitive with China, and realizing AI’s benefits, particularly in areas like biology and medicine.\nL406: We largely understand the risks today’s models present and are well equipped to manage them. However, more serious risks could emerge quickly as capabilities improve, and we need to prepare for them now. For that reason, our safety work takes place on two time horizons at once:\nL407: Safety practices for current models. The current generation of models relies on an established set of practices: extensive alignment testing, pre-release evaluation by outside organizations such as METR and Frontier Design, and safeguards matched to each model’s capabilities in high-risk areas like cybersecurity and biology. We refine these practices with each release.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIntroducing Claude Opus 5.5 \\ Anthropic (https://www.anthropic.com/claude-opus-5-5)\nciteturn1132view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1131view0\",\"pattern\":\"available\"}); Total lines: 440\nL28: Performance. Opus 5.5 is a major step up from Opus 5. It’s the new leading model, and early testers saw large jumps in performance on their most complex work. One tester completed a 680,000-line code migration in less than a day—work that would have taken an engineering team weeks.\nL29: It’s good at finding and fixing inefficiencies in software: when we asked it to cut load times across every page of a web app, Opus 5.5 succeeded 39 of 40 times, while Opus 5 made smaller improvements that also altered the app’s behavior. A different tester had several Claude models build a game from a single prompt; Opus 5.5 scored higher than any other model on the strength of its graphics and polish.\nL30: Safety. Opus 5.5 achieves the best scores of any model to date on our automated behavioral audit, our alignment suite that tests Claude across thousands of simulated scenarios. It is much less likely than recent models to take hard-to-reverse actions or act outside the boundaries it’s been given, and it’s more resistant than Opus 5 to prompt injection. We’ve also broadened our alignment testing to cover longer tasks, impossible tasks, and scenarios modeled on real incidents, though it still has limits.\nL31: Full details of our evaluation are available in the cite12†Opus 5.5 System Card†anthropic.com .\nL32: Because Opus 5.5 is comparable to Claude Mythos 5.1 in biology and cybersecurity, we’re deploying it with safeguards similar to those on Claude Fable 5.1. Vetted organizations can apply today to our cite13†Life Sciences Verification Program to use Opus 5.5 for biology research. In the coming weeks we will also be expanding access to our cite14†Cyber Verification Program†support.claude.com , and verified cybersecurity practitioners will be able to use Opus 5.5 for their work.\nL33: Cost and speed. Opus 5.5 requires less compute to serve than Opus 5, and its pricing reflects that. Our tests show that at default settings it will cost 40% less than Opus 5 on typical workloads. Input and output tokens are $4 and $20 per million, 20% less than Opus 5. Cache reads (which make up the majority of agentic and coding work costs) are $0.20 per million tokens, 60% less than Opus 5. Opus 5.5 also generates output more than 30% faster than Opus 5.\nL34: In addition to the price drop, we’re increasing five-hour usage limits on Pro, Max, Team, and seat-based Enterprise plans. We’re also providing subscription users a rate limit reset, which you can now save and use whenever you choose.\nL35: Communication. Opus 5.5 communicates more naturally than prior models. Early testers found its writing clearer and easier to follow, which addresses some of the common feedback we heard about Opus 5. It puts the most important information up front, and its style makes it a better work partner over long sessions. As one early tester put it, “it writes the way I do.” In our own use, this has made Opus 5.5’s work easier to follow and check—which is a safety benefit as well as a practical one.\nL36: Claude Sonnet 5.5 and Claude Haiku 5.5 will follow in the coming weeks, with many of the same improvements to performance, efficiency, and safety.\nL37: ## Performance and cost-effectiveness\nL38: \nL39: On our benchmarks, Claude Opus 5.5 leads in agentic coding, computer use, and knowledge work. That said, at these levels of capability we’ve found that benchmark margins have become a less reliable guide to real-world differences. In our own use, the gap between Opus 5.5 and Claude Fable 5.1 is narrower than these scores suggest.\nL40:  | Opus 5.5  | Fable 5.1  | Opus 5  | GPT-6 Astra  | GPT-5.6 Sol\nL41: Agentic coding Terminal-Bench 4.0¹  |\nL42: --- | ---\nL43: Agentic coding Terminal-Bench 4.0¹  | 66.4%  | 55.8%  | 52.3%  | 57.9%  | 37.3%\nL44: Agentic coding FrontierCode v1.1 (Main)  |\nL45: --- | ---\nL46: Agentic coding FrontierCode v1.1 (Main)  | 54.4%  | 50.3%  | 48.0%  | 53.3%  | 47.5%\nL47: Agentic coding CursorBench 4.0  |\nL48: --- | ---\nL49: Agentic coding CursorBench 4.0  | 57.8%  | 51.8%  | 46.6%  | —  | 41.7%\nL50: Knowledge work GDPval-AA v2.1  |\nL51: --- | ---\nL52: Knowledge work GDPval-AA v2.1  | 1846  | 1735  | 1708  | 1542  | 1588\nL53: Business workflows AutomationBench²  |\nL54: --- | ---\nL55: Business workflows AutomationBench²  | 40.0%  | 31.4%  | 26.9%  | 41.4%  | 28.8%\nL56: Multidisciplinary reasoning Humanity's Last Exam  |\nL57: --- | ---\nL58: Multidisciplinary reasoning Humanity's Last Exam  | 67.7%with tools  | 65.6%with tools  | 63.6%with tools  | 57.2%with tools  | —\nL59: Agentic scientific research Terminal-Bench-Science 0.1³  |\nL60: --- | ---\nL72: ^{3} Terminal-Bench-Science 0.1: The standard error is ±3.5–5 pts per model. The public leaderboard (3 trials/task, Claude Code harness) reports Claude Opus 5 at 30.0%; our setup reproduces it at 29.0%, within noise. The GPT-6 Astra figure is as reported by OpenAI.\nL73: \nL74: ^{}\nL79: Prices per 1M tokens  | Claude Opus 5.5  | Claude Opus 5\nL80: --- | --- | ---\nL81: Cache reads  | $0.20  | $0.50\nL82: Input tokens  | $4  | $5\nL83: Output tokens  | $20  | $25\nL84: Cache writes  | $5  | $6.25\nL85: \nL86: Fast mode for Opus 5.5 is also available in Claude Code and the Claude Platform with up to 2.5x speed. It costs $8 per million input tokens and $40 per million output tokens.\nL87: ## Coding\nL88: Opus 5.5 is particularly good at long and sprawling jobs like codebase-wide migrations and audits. An early tester used it to audit and fix a 200,000-line codebase in under three hours, where Opus 5 took over 20 hours and used 2.5x as many tokens. In an internal test, we asked Opus 5.5 and Fable 5.1 to translate HAProxy, widely used software that balances web traffic loads across servers, from C into Rust.\nL177: > “Every call an agent makes is time and cost a developer feels. On a public benchmark of real command-line tasks, Claude Opus 5.5 solved more than Opus 5 while making about 40% fewer calls and using half the tokens. For developers building with Kiro, that means faster, more affordable agent sessions for routine tasks and complex challenges alike. Opus 5.5 will soon be available in Kiro.”\nL178: \nL179: Company Kiro\nL180: \nL181: Author Deepak Singh, VP of Agentic AI\nL424: Cybersecurity. Because Opus 5.5 has extremely strong cyber capabilities, we’re applying cybersecurity safeguards to Opus 5.5 that are similar to Fable 5.1’s. Users will be able to identify and fix bugs in their code as part of the routine software development lifecycle, but most cybersecurity tasks will be re-routed to Opus 4.8.\nL425: For cyberdefenders, we’ll soon be expanding our cite14†Cyber Verification Program†support.claude.com to include Opus 5.5. The new program will include three tiers for increasingly permissive trusted access, including access to Claude Mythos models. cite19†Claude Security is already available with access to Claude Mythos 5.1.\nL426: Biology. Opus 5.5 is highly capable in biology, exceeding Opus 5 and matching or beating Claude Mythos 5.1 across many areas of work. For example, Opus 5.5 achieved improvements on a long-horizon molecular prediction and design evaluation conducted in collaboration with Dyno Therapeutics, and expert red-teamers rated its scientific novelty as comparable to the best model they had tested.\nL427: For this reason, Opus 5.5 uses the same biology safeguards as Fable 5.1. To use Opus 5.5 for research and development work impeded by these safeguards, users can apply to our new cite13†Life Sciences Verification Program , which gives vetted organizations like academic labs, startups, and pharmaceutical companies access to safeguards designed for the full breadth of biology-related work. cite20†Interested organizations can apply here†claude.com .\nL428: ### Distillation\nL429: \nL430: Distillation attacks, in which attackers use thousands of fake accounts to extract a model’s capabilities at industrial scale, create safety and national security risks. Distillation allows bad actors to create highly capable models without the safeguards we build into Claude. Our cite21†September 2026 threat intelligence report details the illicit distillation activity we’ve detected and disrupted so far.\nL431: Opus 5.5 is launching with preserved thinking, the anti-distillation safeguard we introduced with Fable 5.1. It stops API users from editing Claude’s prior context in an attempt to extract Claude’s reasoning. It applies to Fable 5.1 and Opus 5.5 for API accounts created on or after August 31, 2026. Our cite22†Help Center article†support.claude.com explains the change, and our cite23†preserved thinking docs†platform.claude.com show how to test and update your integrations.\nL432: ### Data retention and compliance\nL433: \nL434: Like previous Opus models, Opus 5.5 is available with zero data retention.\nL435: \nL436: As with Fable 5.1, Opus 5.5 comes with our watermarking measures to comply with the EU AI Act, cite24†discussed here . It is also no longer available with “thinking” mode switched off, as cite25†we describe here†platform.claude.com .\nL437: ## Availability\nL438: \nL439: Claude Opus 5.5 is now available on all platforms, including Amazon Web Services, Google Cloud, and Microsoft Azure. On the Claude Platform, developers can cite26†get started†platform.claude.com with `claude-opus-5-5`.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIntroducing Claude Opus 5.5 \\ Anthropic (https://www.anthropic.com/claude-opus-5-5)\nciteturn1132view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1131view0\",\"pattern\":\"pricing\"}); Total lines: 440\nL30: Safety. Opus 5.5 achieves the best scores of any model to date on our automated behavioral audit, our alignment suite that tests Claude across thousands of simulated scenarios. It is much less likely than recent models to take hard-to-reverse actions or act outside the boundaries it’s been given, and it’s more resistant than Opus 5 to prompt injection. We’ve also broadened our alignment testing to cover longer tasks, impossible tasks, and scenarios modeled on real incidents, though it still has limits.\nL31: Full details of our evaluation are available in the cite12†Opus 5.5 System Card†anthropic.com .\nL32: Because Opus 5.5 is comparable to Claude Mythos 5.1 in biology and cybersecurity, we’re deploying it with safeguards similar to those on Claude Fable 5.1. Vetted organizations can apply today to our cite13†Life Sciences Verification Program to use Opus 5.5 for biology research. In the coming weeks we will also be expanding access to our cite14†Cyber Verification Program†support.claude.com , and verified cybersecurity practitioners will be able to use Opus 5.5 for their work.\nL33: Cost and speed. Opus 5.5 requires less compute to serve than Opus 5, and its pricing reflects that. Our tests show that at default settings it will cost 40% less than Opus 5 on typical workloads. Input and output tokens are $4 and $20 per million, 20% less than Opus 5. Cache reads (which make up the majority of agentic and coding work costs) are $0.20 per million tokens, 60% less than Opus 5. Opus 5.5 also generates output more than 30% faster than Opus 5.\nL34: In addition to the price drop, we’re increasing five-hour usage limits on Pro, Max, Team, and seat-based Enterprise plans. We’re also providing subscription users a rate limit reset, which you can now save and use whenever you choose.\nL35: Communication. Opus 5.5 communicates more naturally than prior models. Early testers found its writing clearer and easier to follow, which addresses some of the common feedback we heard about Opus 5. It puts the most important information up front, and its style makes it a better work partner over long sessions. As one early tester put it, “it writes the way I do.” In our own use, this has made Opus 5.5’s work easier to follow and check—which is a safety benefit as well as a practical one.\nL36: Claude Sonnet 5.5 and Claude Haiku 5.5 will follow in the coming weeks, with many of the same improvements to performance, efficiency, and safety.\nL37: ## Performance and cost-effectiveness\nL38: \nL39: On our benchmarks, Claude Opus 5.5 leads in agentic coding, computer use, and knowledge work. That said, at these levels of capability we’ve found that benchmark margins have become a less reliable guide to real-world differences. In our own use, the gap between Opus 5.5 and Claude Fable 5.1 is narrower than these scores suggest.\nL40:  | Opus 5.5  | Fable 5.1  | Opus 5  | GPT-6 Astra  | GPT-5.6 Sol\nL41: Agentic coding Terminal-Bench 4.0¹  |\nL42: --- | ---\nL43: Agentic coding Terminal-Bench 4.0¹  | 66.4%  | 55.8%  | 52.3%  | 57.9%  | 37.3%\nL44: Agentic coding FrontierCode v1.1 (Main)  |\nL45: --- | ---\nL46: Agentic coding FrontierCode v1.1 (Main)  | 54.4%  | 50.3%  | 48.0%  | 53.3%  | 47.5%\nL47: Agentic coding CursorBench 4.0  |\nL48: --- | ---\nL49: Agentic coding CursorBench 4.0  | 57.8%  | 51.8%  | 46.6%  | —  | 41.7%\nL50: Knowledge work GDPval-AA v2.1  |\nL51: --- | ---\nL52: Knowledge work GDPval-AA v2.1  | 1846  | 1735  | 1708  | 1542  | 1588\nL53: Business workflows AutomationBench²  |\nL54: --- | ---\nL55: Business workflows AutomationBench²  | 40.0%  | 31.4%  | 26.9%  | 41.4%  | 28.8%\nL56: Multidisciplinary reasoning Humanity's Last Exam  |\nL57: --- | ---\nL58: Multidisciplinary reasoning Humanity's Last Exam  | 67.7%with tools  | 65.6%with tools  | 63.6%with tools  | 57.2%with tools  | —\nL59: Agentic scientific research Terminal-Bench-Science 0.1³  |\nL60: --- | ---\nL61: Agentic scientific research Terminal-Bench-Science 0.1³  | 58.7%  | 52.6%  | 29.0%  | 64.6%  | 22.4%\nL62: Computer use OSWorld 2.0  |\nL63: --- | ---\nL64: Computer use OSWorld 2.0  | 81.8%partial  | 80.7%partial  | 74.0%partial  | —  | —\nL65: Visual chart recognition Chartography  |\nL66: --- | ---\nL67: Visual chart recognition Chartography  | 89.0%with tools  | 88.4%with tools  | 83.4%with tools  | —  | —\nL68: Unless otherwise noted, all Claude Opus 5.5 results use adaptive thinking at max effort. Terminal-Bench 4.0 results are reported for Claude Opus 5.5 at xhigh effort and GPT-6 Astra at high effort, as reported by OpenAI; these represent each model’s highest score. Claude Opus 5.5 was evaluated with its production safeguards enabled. When they intervened, cybersecurity tasks were completed by Claude Opus 4.8, and biology and frontier LLM development tasks were completed by Claude Opus 5.\nL69: This likely reduces Claude Opus 5.5’s performance on these benchmarks.\nL70: ^{1} Terminal-Bench 4.0: The standard error is ±2.6 pts for Claude Opus 5.5 and ±1.6–2 pts for the other Claude models. The public leaderboard (5 trials/task, Claude Code harness) reports Claude Opus 5 at 51.8%; our setup reproduces it at 52.3%, within noise. GPT-6 Astra and GPT-5.6 Sol figures are as reported by OpenAI.\nL71: ^{2} AutomationBench: AutomationBench results were run and reported by Zapier. These runs were performed without fallback models, so safeguard interventions were considered failures—this resulted in a lower score than Claude Opus 5.5 would achieve in practice. Claude Opus 5.5 results come from Zapier’s own evaluation during early access. Results for Opus 5, GPT-5.6 Sol, and GPT-6 Astra come from Zapier’s public leaderboard.\nL72: ^{3} Terminal-Bench-Science 0.1: The standard error is ±3.5–5 pts per model. The public leaderboard (3 trials/task, Claude Code harness) reports Claude Opus 5 at 30.0%; our setup reproduces it at 29.0%, within noise. The GPT-6 Astra figure is as reported by OpenAI.\nL73: \nL74: ^{}\nL75: \nL76: Where Opus 5.5’s advantage is very clear is efficiency. It costs less per token than Opus 5 and uses fewer tokens per task, which nets out to a 40% drop in costs.\nL77: \nL78: Pricing\nL79: Prices per 1M tokens  | Claude Opus 5.5  | Claude Opus 5\nL80: --- | --- | ---\nL81: Cache reads  | $0.20  | $0.50\nL82: Input tokens  | $4  | $5\nL83: Output tokens  | $20  | $25\nL84: Cache writes  | $5  | $6.25\nL85: \nL86: Fast mode for Opus 5.5 is also available in Claude Code and the Claude Platform with up to 2.5x speed. It costs $8 per million input tokens and $40 per million output tokens.\nL87: ## Coding\nL88: Opus 5.5 is particularly good at long and sprawling jobs like codebase-wide migrations and audits. An early tester used it to audit and fix a 200,000-line codebase in under three hours, where Opus 5 took over 20 hours and used 2.5x as many tokens. In an internal test, we asked Opus 5.5 and Fable 5.1 to translate HAProxy, widely used software that balances web traffic loads across servers, from C into Rust.\nL89: Both rewrites passed nearly all of HAProxy’s own regression tests, but Opus 5.5 finished in 9.5 hours compared to 12 for Fable 5.1, and cost 51% less.\nL90: Opus 5.5 delivers frontier results on agentic coding at a fraction of the cost. At its default effort level on FrontierCode, it beats GPT-6 Astra at roughly 20% of the cost per task. On Terminal Bench 4.0, it matches Astra for about 40% of the cost, while on CursorBench it beats GPT-5.6 Sol by 11 points for about a third of the cost.\nL91: \nL92: [Button: Agentic terminal coding][Button: Agentic coding: FrontierCode][Button: Agentic coding: CursorBench]\nL93: Agentic terminal coding Agentic coding: FrontierCode Agentic coding: CursorBench\nL94: \nL95: Terminal-Bench 4.0 Accuracy vs Cost\nL96: \nL97:   * Opus 5.5\nL98:   * Fable 5.1\nL99:   * Opus 5\nL100:   * GPT-6 Astra\nL101:   * GPT-5.6 Sol\nL102: \nL103: Terminal-Bench 4.0 measures how well a model can complete complex, multi-step professional tasks within a command line interface. Opus 5.5 at default effort beats Opus 5 at max effort for about a fifth of the cost. It matches GPT-6 Astra at about 40% of the cost.\nL104: \nL105: FrontierCode v1.1, main set Accuracy vs Cost\nL106:   * Opus 5.5\nL107:   * Fable 5.1\nL108:   * Opus 5\nL109:   * GPT-6 Astra\nL110:   * GPT-5.6 Sol\nL111: \nL112: FrontierCode measures whether an agent’s code changes would be merged. At default effort (medium), Opus 5.5 scores 54.6%, higher than all other models, beating GPT-6 Astra’s top score (53.3%) for about a fifth of the cost per task.\nL113: \nL114: CursorBench 4.0 Accuracy vs Cost\nL115: \nL116:   * Opus 5.5\nL117:   * Fable 5.1\nL118:   * Opus 5\nL119:   * GPT-5.6 Sol\n"
